// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// §1 — LAGRANGE REDUCTION (answers to Q1-Q5)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// VERIFIED AGAINST CODE: operator.rs:122 — logit_s(x) = b_s + Σ_i w_si·x_i
// (sparse linear), and f₀(x) = Σ_s softmax(logits(x);τ)·μ_s exactly.
// Therefore: after ONE deterministic step, x lies in conv{μ_s}. The reduction
// w(t+1) = softmax(A(Σ_r w_r μ_r) + b; τ) is EXACT for the deterministic
// skeleton. Noise σ·η enters only as a perturbation of the logits. This is
// not an approximation — it is a change of coordinates. DS is right.

// CONSISTENCY EVIDENCE DS DID NOT NOTICE: D_eff ≈ 3-5 (Grassberger-Procaccia,
// EXP-TDA-01) matches dim(Δ⁴) = 4. The measured effective dimension of the
// ℝ¹²⁸ dynamics equals the dimension of the weight simplex. This is exactly
// what the reduction predicts, and we measured it BEFORE proposing the
// reduction. This should be the opening argument of any paper on this.

answer(Q1) = {
  q: "Is the simplex reduction the right one, vs Koopman?",
  a: "Yes — prefer the simplex. The softmax reduction is EXACT given the code
      structure; Koopman would be a data-driven approximation of something we
      have in closed form. Koopman remains useful as a CROSS-CHECK: its leading
      eigenfunctions should reconstruct the w_s coordinates. If they don't,
      the noise subspace matters more than we think.",
}

answer(Q2) = {
  q: "What is lost in the reduction?",
  a: "The off-mode-span noise component. TDA says it is contractive
      (β₀=1, β₁=0 under kick), so the slaved dims relax fast. Quantify it:
      reconstruction error ||x(t) − Σ w_s(t)μ_s|| over EXP-TDA-01 data
      (traj_full.npy). If median error is small vs ||x||, the reduction is
      certified. This is step (c) of DS's own pipeline — do it first.",
}

answer(Q3) = {
  q: "τ as bifurcation parameter — how many fixed points?",
  a: "The 5! = 120 Bézout count is WRONG — softmax fixed-point equations are
      not polynomial and Bézout does not apply. The correct frame: this system
      is a GENERALIZED MODERN HOPFIELD NETWORK (Ramsauer et al. 2020,
      'Hopfield Networks is All You Need'). Known results for the symmetric
      case (logit weights = pattern dot-products): for τ below a critical
      value set by pattern separation, up to N=5 stable fixed points near the
      modes plus metastable mixtures; for τ large, a single fixed point near
      uniform. Fold bifurcations at intermediate τ — exactly DS's 'L-points'.
      This gives us established theory to cite AND means the fixed-point
      structure alone is not novel; the VIABILITY overlay (W_Ω polytope in
      the simplex) is the novel part.",
}

answer(Q4) = {
  q: "Does Lyapunov L(x) translate to the simplex?",
  a: "Yes, by direct substitution x = Σ w_s μ_s. L(w) is explicit. Nothing
      deep here — just do it.",
}

answer(Q5) = {
  q: "Is f₀ a gradient system? Is there a potential V(w)?",
  a: "CONDITIONAL YES — this is the most valuable answer in this review.
      Modern Hopfield networks ARE gradient-like: the update minimizes the
      energy E(ξ) = −lse(β, Xᵀξ) + ½ξᵀξ (+const), with convergence proven
      via CCCP (concave-convex procedure). BUT that guarantee needs the
      SYMMETRIC case: logit weights w_si proportional to μ_s,i (dot-product
      attention). Our operator.rs decouples the logit matrix from the modes.
      → ACTION (30 min, DS can do): check whether the deployed logit weights
        are proportional to the mode vectors. If yes: energy function exists,
        cite Ramsauer, convergence is THEOREM not measurement. If no: we have
        a nonsymmetric mode-mixing network — empirical λ_max < 0 stands, and
        constructing its Lyapunov function IS a paper contribution.",
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// §2 — INFORMATION GEOMETRY (answers to Q1-Q4)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

answer(IG_Q1) = {
  q: "Is the natural-gradient derivation correct?",
  a: "NO as stated. The claimed identity — that w(t+1)=softmax(ℓ(w)/τ) is a
      natural-gradient step of F(w)=KL(softmax(ℓ/τ)||w) with step η=τ — does
      not hold: a natural-gradient/mirror-descent step on the simplex produces
      MULTIPLICATIVE-WEIGHTS updates w(t+1)_s ∝ w_s(t)·exp(−η∇_s), which
      retain a factor of the current w. Our map discards w except through
      ℓ(w) — it is a BEST-RESPONSE map, not a gradient step.
      THREE EXACT REPLACEMENTS (all citable):
      (i)  Gibbs variational principle: softmax(ℓ/τ) = argmin_p { −⟨ℓ,p⟩ − τH(p) }.
           Each tick exactly solves an entropy-regularized optimization —
           free-energy minimization per step. This is EXACT and elementary.
      (ii) Continuous-time limit: replicator dynamics IS the natural-gradient
           flow under the Fisher/Shahshahani metric (Shahshahani 1979,
           Hofbauer & Sigmund 1998). Our discrete map is its best-response
           relative. The information-geometry story survives — in the flow,
           not the map.
      (iii) The iteration is fixed-point self-consistent mean-field — same
           family as the Hopfield/CCCP result in §1.",
}

answer(IG_Q2) = {
  q: "Is the auto-EM interpretation solid?",
  a: "YES — this one is exactly right. softmax responsibilities (E-step) +
      convex reconstruction from prototypes (M-step) IS soft k-means, which
      IS EM for an isotropic Gaussian mixture. And idea #7 (learned modes)
      completes it: the prototype update δμ_s = ε·w_s·(x−μ_s) is online EM.
      The kernel with learned modes literally runs online EM on its own state
      stream while using the result to govern itself. Keep this framing —
      it is correct, simple, and reviewers will accept it.",
}

answer(IG_Q3) = {
  q: "Prior work connecting softmax dynamics + natural gradient + homeostatic control?",
  a: "Closest lines: information-theoretic bounded rationality (Ortega & Braun
      2013 — softmax policies from free-energy tradeoffs), active inference
      (Friston — homeostasis as free-energy minimization; our system is a
      MEASURED instance of what that literature mostly simulates), replicator/
      natural-gradient (Shahshahani; Amari). Nobody to my knowledge has the
      combination 'production control kernel + exact simplex reduction +
      measured Fisher-Rao observables'. The COMBINATION is the novelty; the
      ingredients are not.",
}

answer(IG_Q4) = {
  q: "Implement w(t) extraction pipeline now?",
  a: "YES — highest measurement-value-per-effort in the corpus. The Fisher-Rao
      velocity d(t) = arccos(Σ√(w_s(t)w_s(t+1))) as a LEADING indicator of
      excursion (mixture shifts before magnitude deviates) is a testable,
      falsifiable, cheap claim with data already on disk (traj_full.npy,
      3043×128). ~100 lines Python. If it works it goes in AC §19 or NP;
      if it fails, we learn the excursions are noise-driven, which feeds Q2
      of §1. Either outcome is publishable content.",
}

corrections(information_geometry) = [
  "Cheeger constant 'h(Δ⁴) = 2√(log2/π)' — provenance unknown, almost certainly
   fabricated. Delete or derive. The Cheeger PROGRAM (frontier steepness of W_Ω
   bounds recovery time) is fine as a conjecture — the specific number is not.",
  "Sectional curvature 'constant = 2/N' — unverified. The Fisher simplex is
   isometric to a sphere orthant of radius 2 (curvature 1/4) under w_s = q_s²/4
   embedding. Get the constant right or state it qualitatively.",
]

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// §3 — VIABLE COMPRESSOR (answers to Q1-Q5)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

answer(VC_Q1) = {
  q: "Is the conjecture formalizable or just a nice analogy?",
  a: "Formalizable — and largely FORMALIZED already, in literature DS has not
      cited. The exact anchor is COMPUTATIONAL MECHANICS (Crutchfield):
      the minimal sufficient statistic for prediction = causal states =
      ε-machine. VC-1 restated precisely: 'the VSM kernel implements an
      approximate ε-machine for viability-relevant prediction, with Ω-margin
      as the distortion measure.' Rate-distortion control also has a
      literature: Tatikonda & Mitter (control under communication constraints),
      Sims (rational inattention), Ortega & Braun. VC-1 becomes defensible
      the moment it is stated in those terms.",
}

answer(VC_Q2) = {
  q: "Is the Landauer connection worth pursuing?",
  a: "The MEASUREMENT yes, the HYPOTHESIS no. Silicon CPUs dissipate 10⁸-10⁹ ×
      kT·ln2 per logical operation — 'the kernel operates near the Landauer
      limit' is guaranteed false by ~8 orders of magnitude before we measure
      anything. Cut that sentence. What remains valuable: (a) the measured
      bits-per-joule number as a BASELINE for cybernetic regulators (nobody
      has published one); (b) the right citation for the prediction-dissipation
      link is Still, Sivak, Bell & Crooks 2012, 'Thermodynamics of Prediction'
      (PRL) — dissipation = useless nostalgia. That paper says a predictor's
      thermodynamic inefficiency equals the information it retains about the
      past that does not help predict the future. THAT is measurable on our
      kernel (V_mem vs predictive value) and is a much sharper claim than
      Landauer proximity.",
}

answer(VC_Q3) = {
  q: "Is VSL-as-optimal-source-code defensible? How to measure H_sem?",
  a: "Partially. H_tok(VSL) < H_tok(NL) at −5.4% plus 38-79% token compression
      is evidence of a GOOD code, not an OPTIMAL one. Optimality needs a rate
      floor, and 'semantic entropy' has no agreed definition — do not build on
      it. Practical proxy: task-conditional compression — the entropy of the
      spec GIVEN that a competent agent must reconstruct the system from it.
      Measurable as reconstruction fidelity per token (Qwen/DS reconstruct a
      system from spec A vs spec B). Frame as 'near-optimal for its task
      distribution', drop 'absolute'.",
}

answer(VC_Q4) = {
  q: "Should the main paper be about compression rather than viability?",
  a: "No. Viability is the phenomenon; compression is one LENS (P2 already
      owns the substrate/language lens). VC-1 belongs in NP §Discussion as a
      unifying conjecture, not as the headline. Do not reorganize the paper
      program around it.",
}

answer(VC_Q5) = {
  q: "Literature connecting dynamical systems + RG + information theory?",
  a: "Yes: information bottleneck ↔ RG (Tishby et al.; Mehta & Schwab 2014,
      'An exact mapping between the Variational Renormalization Group and
      Deep Learning'). The L0→L1→L2 aggregation chain as coarse-graining that
      preserves viability-relevant information is exactly an information-
      bottleneck RG. Cite Mehta-Schwab; the VSM version (viability as the
      relevance variable) appears genuinely unclaimed.",
}

corrections(viable_compressor) = [
  "INTERNAL CONTRADICTION (E3): 'operates at ~90-95% of entropic capacity'
   and 'compresses to ~50% of h_max' cannot both hold. h_t≈1.9-2.0 bits with
   h_max=2.0 bits (4-state alphabet = 2 bits, not 4) → the system runs at
   ~95-100% of capacity and the 50% claim is an alphabet-size error. Fix E3.",
  "Kolmogorov 10× ratio (3MB trajectory / 300KB source) is category-confused
   for a STOCHASTIC system: K(trajectory) ≈ |program| + H(noise sequence),
   and the noise term dominates. The gzip test DS proposed is the right move —
   run it and report the measured ratio instead of the bound.",
]

